## 1. Add the mechanistic argument (Mechanism, 25 marks)

Every rejection in `hypotheses.yaml` is purely statistical. For M6 it even says optimiser limits "prevent an absolute rejection proof." The data give a structural proof that doesn't depend on the optimiser:

- **There is a hidden intermediate.** B0 − B − P, the material that has left B but not reached P, rises to about 0.10 M at 30–90 min in every run, then falls back to zero. That is roughly 15–20× the combined assay noise. Q is only about 3e-4 M, so it can't account for the gap.
  - M1, M3 and M5 have no intermediate, so they require B0 − B − P to equal Q. They're out.
- **M6 fails twice.**
  - In M6, B is consumed only in I + B → P, so the gap must be zero at all times.
  - A → I doesn't need B, so A should keep falling after B runs out. Instead A sits flat at about 0.75 M from roughly 300 to 960 min.
- **Why M2 and M4 tie.** A only moves from 1.0 to 0.75 M in every run, so M4's k3·[A] behaves like a constant, which is exactly M2's rate law. The difference M4 predicts in the Q/P ratio across a run is about 15–20 %, the same size as the Q assay noise.
  - This shows the tie comes from the experimental design, not from a fitting failure.

**Suggested changes:**
- **New figure:** plot B0 − B − P against time for all five runs, with a ±2σ band.
- **Justification text:** rewrite it in plain language and keep the likelihood numbers as supporting evidence.
- **Link to System A:** the one System A miss was picking an intermediate model when the truth was direct. This figure is what shows System B's intermediate isn't that same mistake.
- **Weights:** present 0.5/0.5 as their posterior-equivalent weight (AICc gives 0.51/0.49 with equal priors). The brief asks for exactly that, and the "NOT posterior probabilities" disclaimers undersell it.

## 2. Spread the campaign across temperature (Campaign, 20 marks)

All three runs sit at 75 °C. I reran their own uncertainty and simulation code on two alternatives:

| Design | A used | Samples | SD Ea1 | SD Ea2 | SD Ea3 | SD (Ea3−Ea2) | Correct model picked |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Released data only | – | – | 1.8 | 3.3 | 8.1 | 7.5 | – |
| Team's: 3 runs, all at 75 °C | 3.13 g | 21 | 1.8 | 3.3 | 8.0 | 7.5 | 100 % |
| Same 3 charges at 55/100/120 °C | 3.13 g | 21 | 0.64 | 1.06 | 1.8–2.1 | 1.6–2.0 | 100 % |
| 6 runs, temperature × charge | 4.69 g | 42 | 0.38 | 0.70 | 1.3–1.8 | 1.2–1.7 | 100 % |

Uncertainties are in kJ/mol; where two numbers appear, the range covers M2 and M4. Discrimination comes from their own simulation audit.

- **It costs nothing.** Moving the same three charges to different temperatures keeps the material, the sample count and the 100 % discrimination. It also cuts the activation-energy uncertainty about fourfold.
- **Why that matters.** Ea3 − Ea2 decides how the Q:P split responds to temperature drift, which is the "what happens when parameters drift" question under Quality by Design. At the moment they can't even tell its sign: about −1 ± 7.5 kJ/mol.
- **Their rationale undersells the gap.** "Temperature already varies in the released data" means only 65–85 °C, out of the allowed 50–120 °C.
- **Discrimination isn't the binding constraint.** Even their worst-case simulated campaign favours the true model by a log-likelihood margin of 141. What matters now is information per gram. Say this explicitly whichever design they keep.
- **Sampling waste.**
  - E1 is finished by about 240 min, so the 360, 480, 720 and 960 min samples re-read the same plateau.
  - Each run spends its first sample at t = 0, which their own fit discards. They should either drop it or justify it as a check on the initial charge.

Caveats on my numbers: the precision figures use a linearised (Fisher-information) approximation that ignores censoring. Sample times were chosen by their own rule at each temperature. The 6-run design is illustrative, not optimised.

## 3. State the control-strategy consequence (Justification, 20 marks)

Their own predictions already contain this, but no deliverable says it:

- **Under M2:** crude Q/P is about 1,020 ppm (molar) at every held-out charge and temperature. Nothing in the reaction controls Q, so downstream purging has to do roughly 70× to reach 15 ppm.
- **Under M4:** Q/P falls to about 400 ppm at a 1:1 charge and about 150 ppm when A is limiting (H11). Stoichiometry is the lever, and the 4:1 ratio carried over from discovery is the worst case.
- **The w/w spec depends on the mechanism too.** Under M4, Q carries an extra A, so it's 250 g/mol heavier than P.

This is the brief's "M4 says control your stoichiometry," quantified, and it's what makes the campaign matter.

